{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgLzZrHbmGskLZJ47D2aH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JYsBw5Z8AVRjEVukkV9C3j","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"fix_prompt":"# Repair plan for /playwright/playwright:<startup>\n\n## Diagnosis\n\n`docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` declares one service, `frontend`, at\n`services/frontend` (typescript, next.js + react). **That directory does not\nexist — there is no `services/` tree in the repo at all.** The only writable\nroot is `services/**` (`.claude/settings.json` allows exactly\n`Write/Edit(services/**)`).\n\nThe harness chain is:\n\n1. `tests/playwright.config.ts` derives a per-worktree host port from\n   `sha256(process.cwd())` and sets `use.baseURL =\n   http://127.0.0.1:<port>`.\n2. Its `webServer` block runs `docker compose up --build frontend` with\n   `cwd: '..'` (repo root), `env: { FRONTEND_PORT: <port>,\n   COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME: bdd-<digest> }`, and waits up to 300 s for\n   `http://127.0.0.1:<port>` to answer.\n3. `docker-compose.yaml` defines `frontend` with `build.context:\n   ./services/frontend` and maps `\"${FRONTEND_PORT:-3000}:3000\"` — i.e. **the\n   app must listen on port 3000 inside the container**.\n\nBecause `./services/frontend` is missing, `docker compose up --build` aborts\nimmediately, nothing ever binds the port, and Playwright fails during\n`webServer` startup. A startup failure yields no per-test results, which is\nwhy the failing set surfaces as the synthetic test id `<startup>` with an\nempty failure group.\n\nSo this is not a bug to patch — the entire production service has to be\nbuilt. Nothing in `tests/`, `docker-compose.yaml` or `docs/` may be touched;\nall work lands under `services/frontend/`.\n\n## Contract the service must satisfy\n\n- **Buildable compose context.** `services/frontend/` must contain a\n  `Dockerfile` that `docker compose up --build frontend` can build with no\n  extra build args, no host-provided env, and no files from outside that\n  directory.\n- **Listens on 3000, on all interfaces.** The compose port mapping publishes\n  container `3000` to the host port Playwright polls. The process must bind\n  `0.0.0.0:3000` (not `127.0.0.1` inside the container, or the published port\n  answers with a connection reset and Playwright times out after 300 s).\n- **`GET /` returns HTTP 200** so Playwright's URL readiness probe resolves,\n  and the container must stay up for the duration of the test run (it is a\n  foreground `docker compose up`, not detached).\n- **`GET /` renders exactly one `<h1>` whose text is `Hello world`.**\n  `tests/integration/frontend-home.spec.ts` does\n  `page.goto('/')` then\n  `expect(page.locator('h1')).toHaveText('Hello world')`. `toHaveText` with a\n  string is an exact match on normalised text, and the locator runs in strict\n  mode — a second `h1` anywhere in the document (including one injected by the\n  root layout or a dev-mode overlay) is a strict-mode violation. One `h1`,\n  text exactly `Hello world`, no extra nested markup inside it.\n- **Next.js App Router, server components by default.** The architecture's\n  `component-composition` pattern says server components by default and\n  `'use client'` only when hooks or browser APIs are needed. The home page\n  needs neither, so no `'use client'` anywhere in this change.\n- **`services/frontend/tsconfig.json` must exist**, because the architecture\n  declares it as the `typescript` code quality gate's config.\n\n## Files to create\n\nAll paths relative to the repo root. Every one of these is new.\n\n### 1. `services/frontend/package.json`\nPrivate package for the service. Scripts must include `build` and a `start`\nthat binds the right host/port, e.g. `next start -H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000`\n(`-H 0.0.0.0` explicitly, so a stray `HOSTNAME` env in the base image cannot\nmake Next bind the wrong interface). Dependencies: `next`, `react`,\n`react-dom`. Dev dependencies: `typescript`, `@types/node`, `@types/react`,\n`@types/react-dom`.\n\nPin **exact** versions (no `^`/`~`) for `next`, `react` and `react-dom` so the\nimage build is reproducible and a future major cannot silently break the\nstartup path. Pick a Next 15.x release with the matching React 19.x pair it is\ntested against, and a Node base image satisfying Next's engine floor (Node 20\nor 22 — do not use Node 18).\n\n*Serves:* `<startup>` (without this, the Dockerfile has nothing to install or\nrun).\n\n### 2. `services/frontend/Dockerfile`\nKeep it single-stage and boring — every extra stage is another way for\n`<startup>` to fail. Intended shape:\n\n- `FROM node:22-alpine` (or the chosen LTS alpine tag), `WORKDIR /app`.\n- Copy the manifest(s) first, install dependencies, then copy the rest of the\n  service and run `npm run build`.\n- `EXPOSE 3000`, `CMD` running the `start` script.\n\nDependency install: if you also commit a `package-lock.json` (preferred —\ngenerate it by running `npm install` inside `services/frontend/` and keep the\nresulting lockfile), copy it alongside `package.json` and use\n`npm ci --no-audit --no-fund`. If no lockfile is committed, use\n`npm install --no-audit --no-fund` — do **not** write a `npm ci` that will\nabort because the lockfile is absent.\n\nDo not add `output: 'standalone'` plumbing; it adds copy steps that are easy\nto get wrong and buys nothing here.\n\n*Serves:* `<startup>` — this is the file whose absence currently kills the\nrun.\n\n### 3. `services/frontend/.dockerignore`\nExclude `node_modules`, `.next`, and any local build output. The build context\nis `services/frontend` itself, so if you generate a lockfile by running\n`npm install` on the host, the host `node_modules` would otherwise be copied\ninto the image and can shadow the container install (wrong platform binaries\nfor `next`/`sharp` → build or start failure).\n\n*Serves:* `<startup>` (robustness of the image build).\n\n### 4. `services/frontend/next.config.mjs`\nMinimal config o